WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:04.430
For every gripping, well-crafted piece of
cinema released in a given year, it seems

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:08.760
there must be at least one misguided, no-good
slog of a film released to match it.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:13.260
2019 is no exception and these are some of
the worst movies of the bunch.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:18.680
Overcomer is the latest effort from brothers
Alex and Stephen Kendrick, purveyors of faith-based

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.180
flicks such as Fireproof, Courageous, and
War Room.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:25.820
While religious dramas haven't exactly earned
a reputation for being critical darlings,

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:30.340
Overcomer still managed to fall well below
expectations, owing to everything from its

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:34.460
clunky title to its stilted performances to
its relentless proselytizing.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:39.570
Mark Dujsik of RogerEbert.com channeled the
site's namesake in dismantling the movie for

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:43.020
its steadfast refusal to be well, to be an
actual movie.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:43.540
He wrote:

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:45.800
"There's about half a movie in Overcomer.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.280
The other half or so is a pretty half-hearted
sermon.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:53.040
Neither half is particularly worthwhile, and
the whole is cheap, cheesy, and, to put it

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.340
charitably, churchy."

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.960
Variety's Nick Schager concurred, opining:

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:02.360
"[Overcomer is] a drama that affects sensitivity
while nonetheless operating as a blunt instrument.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:06.880
Its one-note sermonizing should help it appeal
to its target audience, but those not already

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.940
in the fold will likely be left unmoved."

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:13.660
It's an aesthetic that has come to be expected
from faith-based films, but the biggest critical

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:18.200
burn was levied by Movie Nation's Roger Moore,
who was most offended by the Kendrick brothers'

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:19.860
failure to respect their craft.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:20.580
He wrote:

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:22.320
"There's just nothing here...

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:27.400
Even as comfort food for true believers, Overcomer
cannot overcome its myriad shortcomings."

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:31.920
Angel Has Fallen is the third in a series
of films following the exploits of Secret

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:38.220
Service agent Mike Banning, preceded by 2013's
Olympus Has Fallen and 2016's London Has Fallen.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:43.200
How a third installment was warranted is anybody's
guess, since neither film scored with critics

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.120
or cleaned up at the box office.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:49.080
The series as a whole just seems to exist
at this point to serve as a way to poke fun

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:50.020
at Gerard Butler.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:57.060
"It’s funny ha?

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.360
It’s funny I know I get it."

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:04.619
Angel continued the films' tradition of lazily
plotted, half-heartedly shot action and intrigue

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:09.200
and its paint-by-numbers aesthetic was judged
by the vast majority of critics to be an insult

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:14.100
to any action fans who require at least a
modicum of personal investment from filmmakers.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:18.500
Flat characters and nonsensical plotting can
often be forgiven if the action component

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:23.180
delivers, but director Ric Roman Waugh's uninspired
staging and photography was the final nail

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:24.460
in the coffin.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.340
But if one thing can be said for Angel Has
Fallen, the movie at least brought out some

00:02:28.349 --> 00:02:32.940
decent snark in virtually every critic who
saw it including FilmWeek's Claudia Pugh,

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:33.520
who wrote:

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:35.960
"The franchise has fallen, and it can't get
up."

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:41.400
Another year, another mess of a movie starring
John Travolta, who at this point seems so

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:46.080
unbelievably far from his Pulp Fiction glory
days that many fans are probably beginning

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.560
to wonder if he ever actually was in that
movie.

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:53.060
The Fanatic stars Travolta in an autistic
man who goes completely off the rails after

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:54.940
being rebuffed by his favorite actor.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:58.200
But is this story handled with any sensitivity
or nuance?

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:03.060
Well, for readers who were fans of popular
music in the late '90s and early '00s, the

00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:07.150
fact that this film was co-written and directed
by Limp Bizkit's Fred Durst should tell you

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:09.440
everything you need to know about its sensibilities.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:15.050
Robert Abele of The Wrap penned a particularly
brutal takedown of the film, seeming to take

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:17.080
its very existence as a personal affront.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:17.880
He wrote:

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.260
"[The Fanatic] is a brainless, exploitative
folly which gives John Travolta free rein

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:27.170
to mine the history of cringe-worthy autism
portrayals for an offensively garish Frankenstein

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:28.970
pantomime of unhinged obsession."

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.690
John J. Rambo might have been responsible
for roughly as many box office dollars as

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:38.340
he was dead bad guys, but nobody has ever
really seen his movies as anything other than

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:41.900
mindless, disposable action flicks… with
the sole exception of First Blood.

00:03:42.209 --> 00:03:46.299
The Rambo series has also been increasingly
burdened by troublesome political overtones

00:03:46.299 --> 00:03:50.590
over the years, and with Rambo: Last Blood,
the series hit its nadir prompting the vast

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:53.800
majority of the critics who viewed it to savage
it in their reviews.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:58.180
Critics found the film's depiction of Mexico
to be particularly ill-advised and that's

00:03:58.180 --> 00:03:59.060
putting it kindly.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.040
Salon's Matthew Rozsa wrote:

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:05.540
"[The movie] is less an escapist action movie
and more a dramatized manifestation of the

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:09.760
most notorious sentences from Donald Trump's
presidential campaign announcement speech...

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:14.510
When you take action film genre tropes and
graft a political narrative onto them, people

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:17.340
often imbibe the ideology along with the spectacle.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:19.380
That is when they become propaganda."

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.500
Rozsa's assessment wasn't even the most blunt
or the most damning Matthew Turner of The

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:24.160
List wrote:

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:28.820
"The horrific finale makes Rambo himself seem
like the real monster, making you briefly

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:30.960
wonder if Stallone is aware of the irony.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:33.000
On balance, one suspects not."

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:38.620
It's never going to be easy adapting an acclaimed,
Pulitzer Prize-winning novel for the screen,

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:42.620
but Donna Tartt's The Goldfinch was always
going to be trickier than most owing to its

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:45.020
hugely complex, thematically-dense narrative.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:49.600
Unfortunately, the material proved to be too
heavy for director John Crowley and screenwriter

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:53.880
Peter Straughan, who squandered the talents
of a heavyweight cast in service of a flat

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:56.130
and inexcusably boring adaptation.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:00.080
It wasn't that the filmmakers didn't follow
the blueprint laid out in the novel; it was

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:02.880
just the lack of skill and finesse with which
they did so.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:07.140
The Atlantic's David Sims summed up the general
consensus nicely, writing:

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:11.580
"Watching The Goldfinch is like having the
plot of a novel read to you not the novel

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:16.800
itself, but merely its long and winding synopsis,
a bitesize summary that still manages to feel

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:17.460
endless."

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:22.039
Most reviewers agreed that the flick's meandering
pace, dearth of emotion, and assembly line

00:05:22.039 --> 00:05:26.720
feel made it a challenging watch in all the
wrong ways but perhaps the most painful zinger

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:29.240
was lobbed by Graeme Tuckett of Stuff, who
wrote:

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:31.820
"[The Goldfinch] is a facsimile of a good
film.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.540
Or perhaps a parody of one."

